.TH DNS 1
.SH NAME
dns \- resolve hostnames over DNS-over-HTTPS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dns
.RB [ -6 ]
.RB [ -s
.IR resolver-ip ]
.RB [ -u
.IR resolver-url ]
.IR name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Dns
resolves each
.I name
over DNS-over-HTTPS
.RI ( doh (2)),
printing a line
.RI \(lq name\ address \(rq
per address. Because it resolves over this tree's own TLS through a
resolver reached by literal IP, it needs no
.I /net/cs
and no host resolver \(em it is the first command in the tree that
can turn a name into an address by itself.
.PP
.B -6
asks for AAAA (IPv6) records instead of A.
.B -s
and
.B -u
point at a different DoH provider (its dial IP and its
.B /dns-query
URL); by default it uses Google Public DNS
.RB ( dns.google
at
.BR 8.8.8.8 ).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/dns.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR doh (2),
.IR dohtest (1),
.IR dial (2)
.SH BUGS
Live resolution is currently blocked at the TLS layer, not in this
command: every public DoH resolver serves an ECDSA certificate, and
.IR tls12 (2)
verifies RSA chains only, so the handshake fails before any query is
sent. The command and its
.IR doh (2)
backend are complete and the wire format is verified offline
.RI ( dohtest (1)); resolution
will work unchanged once ECDSA support lands in the TLS stack. See
.IR doh (2)'s
BUGS and
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md .
